#: ../../intro.rst:2
msgid "Hermes in one page"
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:5
msgid "Hermes and PyHermes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:7
msgid ""
"**Hermes** is an in situ multiresolution framework for cosmic statistics."
" **PyHermes** is its open-source Python implementation. The framework "
"replaces repeated ex situ enumeration of particle pairs or tuples with "
"algebraic operations among window-filtered continuous fields."
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:12
msgid "The central workflow is deliberately small:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:14
msgid "A catalogue is projected onto a compact scaling-function basis."
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:15
msgid "A ``WindowFunc`` describes the spatial operation required by a statistic."
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:16
msgid "``SFCField @ WindowFunc`` constructs a filtered field by FFT convolution."
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:17
msgid ""
"Products and spatial averages of filtered fields produce the requested "
"count-level quantity or connected statistic."
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:20
msgid ""
"The important consequence is reuse. Once a catalogue has become an "
"``SFCField``, changing a separation bin, smoothing scale, multipole "
"projector, or differential operator does not require projecting the "
"catalogue again."
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:25
msgid "Why a field--window language?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:27
msgid ""
"Conventional estimators often arrive as independent counting algorithms: "
"a spherical shell for an isotropic 2PCF, rings for a redshift-space 2PCF,"
" rotated configurations for a standard 3PCF, and spherical harmonics for "
"3PCF multipoles. Hermes treats these as different windows acting on the "
"same field."
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:32
msgid "This viewpoint offers three practical advantages:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:34
msgid "**Reuse**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:35
msgid ""
"The catalogue-to-field projection is performed once. The same field can "
"be used for one-point, two-point, three-point, weighted, and differential"
" analyses."
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:39
msgid "**Flexibility**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:40
msgid ""
"Binning is no longer restricted to one geometric convention. Thin shells,"
" thick shells, Gaussian shells, rings, disks, cylindrical windows, and "
"user-defined Fourier kernels use the same interface."
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:44
msgid "**Scalability**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:45
msgid ""
"FFT window operations scale primarily with the number of MRA coefficients"
" and requested windows, rather than directly with the number of catalogue"
" pairs or triplets. MPI, Numba threads, and an optional CUDA summation "
"backend are available for the expensive workflows."
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:51
msgid "What PyHermes currently computes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:53
msgid "The current release provides:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:55
msgid "``SFCProjection`` for catalogue-to-field reconstruction;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:56
msgid "``Counting`` for random-position sampling and one-point PDFs;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:57
msgid "``Corr_2PCF`` for isotropic and anisotropic two-point statistics;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:58
msgid ""
"``Corr_3PCF`` for Monte Carlo standard 3PCFs with particle or box-random "
"centres;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:60
msgid "``Corr_3PCF_Multipole`` for 3PCF multipoles and radial scans;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:61
msgid "catalogue weights, physical field values, and marked fields;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:62
msgid ""
"smoothing, binning, high-pass, differential, and inverse-Laplacian "
"windows;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:63
msgid "CPU and GPU backends for the final 3PCF-multipole contraction."
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:65
msgid ""
"The examples in the paper use periodic simulation boxes. Explicit random "
"fields can represent non-uniform reference catalogues, but a complete "
"survey analysis must still supply its own mask, selection function, and "
"systematic weights. PyHermes provides the field and estimator machinery; "
"it does not guess an observational selection function for you."
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:72
msgid "The four layers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:75
msgid "Catalogue layer"
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:77
msgid ""
"Input positions may come from in-memory arrays or from raw binary, NumPy "
"NPZ, Gadget binary, Gadget HDF5, and FoF readers. ``catalog_weight`` "
"represents a selection or catalogue weight, while ``field_value`` carries"
" a mark or a physical quantity such as mass or one velocity component."
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:83
msgid "MRA-field layer"
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:85
msgid ""
"``SFCProjection`` returns an ``SFCField`` with coefficients ``epsilon``. "
"The multiresolution level ``J`` gives ``L = 2**J`` coefficients per axis "
"and ``2**(3*J)`` coefficients in three dimensions. The default basis is "
"Daubechies D4, named ``db2`` by PyWavelets."
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:91
msgid "Window layer"
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:93
msgid ""
"``WindowFunc`` stores a Fourier-space kernel compatible with one MRA "
"field. Windows fall into four roles:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:96
msgid "smoothing windows define a local average or filter;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:97
msgid "binning windows define pair or triangle separation support;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:98
msgid "multipole windows project angular structure;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:99
msgid ""
"operator windows apply derivatives, the Laplacian, or the inverse "
"Laplacian."
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:102
msgid "Task layer"
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:104
msgid ""
"Task classes load or accept fields, apply the requested windows, compute "
"the necessary field products, and save typed result objects such as "
"``Corr2PCFData`` or ``Corr3PCFMultipoleData``. The task interface is the "
"usual production route; direct field arithmetic is invaluable for "
"understanding and extending an estimator."
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:111
msgid "Numerical scope"
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:113
msgid ""
"Hermes is not a promise that every one-off measurement is faster than "
"every specialised counter. For a small catalogue and one conventional "
"statistic, a highly tuned pair counter may be the simpler tool. PyHermes "
"is strongest when the reconstructed field is reused, when windows are "
"non-standard, when many configurations are required, or when higher-order"
" tuple counting would become the dominant cost."
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:120
msgid ""
"Finite ``J`` also means finite spatial resolution. If the MRA cell size "
"``box_size / 2**J`` is comparable to the requested separation or bin "
"width, small-scale amplitudes will be suppressed. Convergence in ``J`` is"
" therefore a scientific check, not merely a performance setting."
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:126
msgid "Terminology used in this guide"
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:128
msgid "``SFCField``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:129
msgid ""
"A field represented by scaling-function coefficients. ``SFC`` means "
"scaling-function coefficients."
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:132
msgid "``SFCProjection``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:133
msgid "The catalogue-to-field projection task."
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:135
msgid "``window``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:136
msgid "An optional smoothing or physical operator applied to an input vertex."
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:138
msgid "``binning_window``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:139
msgid ""
"A window whose parameters are mapped from sampled pair or triangle "
"coordinates. Older names such as \"pair window\" are not used."
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:142
msgid "``filtered field``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:143
msgid "The result of applying a window to an ``SFCField``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:145
msgid "``product``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../intro.rst:146
msgid ""
"Either an intermediate count-level field product, such as ``dd`` or "
"``delta_ddd_l``, or a final statistic, such as ``xi``, ``Q``, or "
"``zeta_l``."
msgstr ""

